#!/bin/sh
# Said here as well as in the control file's Description, because a package
# manager is where a false promise costs most: someone installs, launches,
# gets a dialog they did not expect, and files a bug against a maintainer
# rather than reading a README. The same note ships in
# packaging/aur/cordial-git/cordial-git.install for the same reason.
set -e

case "$1" in
    configure)
        cat <<'MSG'

Cordial ships no Roblox build. On first run it offers to fetch one, and
installs it only if Roblox's own signing certificate signed it.

Roblox publishes no Android binary of its own -- its endpoint answers
supportsAndroidBinaries: false -- so the build comes from a third-party
mirror, and Cordial keeps its own copy of it. If you would rather use Sober's
build, or an APK you already have, Settings > Roblox can copy either in. Cordial
does not follow Sober's updates after that.

Start it by typing `cordial`, or from your application list. `cordial-run`
is the loader it launches and is not what you run by hand.

https://github.com/luohoa97/cordial#readme says which claims were measured
and how.

MSG
        ;;
esac

exit 0
